The results demonstrate that mobile phone usage during driving is affected by both vehicle-related and behavioural factors. Drivers possessing public or commercial licenses, operating older vehicles, or having prior accident experience exhibited a higher propensity to use mobile phones. Conversely, precautionary
measures, including activating silent mode or refraining from phone use in high-risk areas, diminished this likelihood. Responses to incoming call, whether through hand-free methods or by pausing to answer, also influenced phone usage. The findings indicate that socio-cultural norms and individual attitudes towards


